<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Cirksena</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cirksena</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cirksena were a family of East Frisian chieftains who united most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> in the 15th century. In 1464 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-i-count-of-east-frisia">Ulrich Cirksena</a> was made imperial count, and his descendants ruled the County, later Principality, of East Frisia until the line died out in 1744, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> took over. Through marriage they also held the Westphalian county of Rietberg in the 16th and 17th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-cirksena.jpg" alt="House of Cirksena" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wappen erstellt von LeoDavid · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cirksena-Stamm-Wappen.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Cirksena descended from a line of chieftains of Greetsiel. Their name is of Frisian origin, probably from the old forename Tzirk or Cirk, and is still a common family name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a>; Enno Attena took it on his marriage to the heiress Gela Syardsna of Manslagt. In 1453, after clashes between rival lines of chieftains, the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emden">Emden</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> had placed under direct rule in 1439, was given to the family, which ruled it until 1595.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The family rose by succeeding the tom Brok chieftains after their opponent Focko Ukena was defeated and expelled by allied chieftains led by Edzard Cirksena. Emperor Frederick III raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-i-count-of-east-frisia">Ulrich Cirksena</a> to imperial count and enfeoffed him with the Imperial County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a>. Under Edzard I (1462–1528), the most important ruler of the house, the county grew to its widest extent and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> spread through it. In 1654 the emperor made the Cirksena princes.</p><p>Charles Edzard, the last ruler of the house, died without issue in the night of 25 to 26 May 1744, reportedly from a glass of buttermilk drunk after a hunt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> took over the state at once. A second line had meanwhile held Rietberg. Count Enno III gained it by marrying its heiress, Walburg von Rietberg, but ceded it to his daughters in the Treaty of Berum of 1600. In 1601 his brother, Count John III, married Enno's daughter Sabina Catherine with papal dispensation; both converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, founding a Catholic branch whose last male, Count Ferdinand Maximilian, died in 1687.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The family's arms, a crowned golden harpy on a black field, outlived it. Count Rudolf Christian placed the harpy in the comital arms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> in 1625, and these are still the emblem of East Frisia. The harpy also appears in the arms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emden">Emden</a>, of Delfzijl opposite it, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurich">Aurich</a> district and of Krummhörn, the family's ancestral homeland. Through the marriage of Agnes Cirksena to Gundakar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>, it is part of the arms of the Principality of Liechtenstein.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cirksena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Cirksena</b>, raised to princes in the 17th century, ruled from Aurich, but the estates, above all the town of Emden, held great power.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-east-frisia">County of East Frisia</a></li><li><blockquote>Through marriage it passed in the 16th century to the East Frisian <b>Cirksena</b> family and then, in 1699, to the Kaunitz family, whose most famous member, Wenzel Anton von Kaunitz-Rietberg, became Austrian state chancellor under Maria Theresa.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-rietberg">County of Rietberg</a></li><li><blockquote>East Frisia came under the <b>Cirksena</b> counts in 1464, and in 1498 Emperor Maximilian made Albert of Saxony governor of Friesland, ending the freedom west of the Ems and creating the Lordship of Frisia.</blockquote><a href="/state/frisian-freedom">Frisian Freedom</a></li><li><blockquote>Edo Wiemken the Elder, active from about 1359, built Jever into a lordship, and his descendants resisted the ambitions of the <b>Cirksena</b> rulers of the County of East Frisia.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-jever">Lordship of Jever</a></li></ul></section>
